    <!-- Content -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic">
          An RV power adapter changes the shape of a plug. It does not change how much power the outlet can supply, and it does not protect anything. Those two facts explain almost every RV electrical problem we hear about.
        </p>

        <QuickPicks products={PRODUCTS} />

        <div id="at-a-glance" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. The RV Adapters at a Glance
          </h2>

          <p>
            Shore power in North America comes in three common shapes. Campground pedestals usually offer a TT-30 receptacle for 30-amp RVs, a NEMA 14-50 for 50-amp RVs, and an ordinary 5-15 or 5-20 household-style outlet. Your RV's power cord ends in one of the first two. Whenever the plug on your cord does not match the receptacle in front of you, you need an RV adapter, and the short, heavy-duty pigtail style most campers use is called a dogbone adapter because of its shape.
          </p>

          <p>
            That is the first category in this guide. The second is the RV surge protector, which plugs into the pedestal before your cord and either blocks voltage spikes or, in the more capable versions called energy management systems (EMS), disconnects the RV when the pedestal's wiring or voltage is wrong. If you buy only one piece of RV electrical gear beyond the cord, buy the protector. Adapters are cheap; a fried converter, refrigerator board or air conditioner is not.
          </p>

          <p>
            We picked six widely sold products to cover both jobs: three Camco PowerGrip dogbones for the conversions most owners need, Progressive Industries' portable EMS and surge-only protectors, and Hughes' Power Watchdog. These picks are based on published specifications and documentation, not on our own bench testing, and where we say how a product behaves we attribute it to the maker or to what owners commonly report.
          </p>

          <p>
            Before you plug anything in, check the pedestal breaker is off, look at the receptacle for scorch marks or loose fit, and plug in with the breaker off before turning it on. Reverse the order when you leave. Making and breaking a 30- or 50-amp connection under load is what pits and burns plug blades, and burned blades are the most common reason adapters and cords overheat.
          </p>

          <p>
            If you are setting up power at home rather than at a campground, read the dryer-outlet section before buying anything, and plan on a licensed electrician. A properly installed RV receptacle is not expensive, and it is the only arrangement we recommend for regular use. For outage planning, the RV itself is not a backup generator for the house; our <a href="/best-home-generator-for-power-outages/">home generator guide</a> covers the right ways to do that.
          </p>
        </div>

