        <div id="plugs" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. TT-30 vs 14-50 vs 5-15: Know Your Plugs
          </h2>

          <p>
            A TT-30 is a 120-volt, 30-amp, three-prong receptacle: one hot, one neutral and one ground. It is the standard for most travel trailers, smaller fifth wheels and Class C motorhomes, and it can supply up to about 3,600 watts. The TT stands for travel trailer. It looks a little like a dryer outlet, but it is wired completely differently, which is the source of the most dangerous mistake in RV electrical work.
          </p>

          <p>
            A NEMA 14-50 is a 50-amp, four-prong receptacle with two hot legs, a neutral and a ground. It supplies 120 volts on each leg and 240 volts between them. A 50-amp RV uses the two legs as two separate 120-volt, 50-amp supplies, which is why it can run two air conditioners plus a long list of appliances: up to about 12,000 watts total. The same receptacle is used for many electric ranges and for <a href="/best-portable-ev-charger/">portable EV chargers</a>, which is why it is increasingly common in garages.
          </p>

          <p>
            A NEMA 5-15 is the ordinary household outlet, rated for 15 amps at 120 volts, or about 1,800 watts, and much less than that for continuous loads. A 5-20, with one T-shaped slot, is the 20-amp version, good for about 2,400 watts. A 5-15 plug fits a 5-20 receptacle, but not the other way around. Most of the confusion in the adapter aisle comes from people trying to connect a 30- or 50-amp RV to one of these.
          </p>

