          <p>
            Order of connection matters. The pedestal feeds the protector, the protector feeds the adapter if you need one, and the adapter feeds the RV cord. Putting the adapter between the pedestal and the protector also works for most combinations, but a 30-amp protector cannot plug directly into a 50-amp pedestal, so the 50M/30F dogbone goes first in that case. Either way, the protector still sees the RV's load and supply voltage.
          </p>

          <p>
            Fifty-amp owners should buy the 50-amp versions of these protectors, such as Progressive's EMS-PT50X and the 50-amp Power Watchdog, because a 30-amp unit cannot pass two-leg power. If your 50-amp RV must use a 30-amp pedestal with a 30M/50F adapter, both legs share one 30-amp supply, so you are limited to about 3,600 watts total and one air conditioner at most.
          </p>

          <p>
            Inspect every adapter before each trip. Look for discoloration around the blades, a loose fit in the receptacle, cracked housings or melted plastic. Any of those is a reason to throw it away. Dogbones are cheap compared with the damage a heat-failed connection can do, and the PowerGrip-style handles exist to let you pull the plug out by the body rather than the cord.
          </p>
