        <div id="which" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            9. Which RV Adapter Should You Buy?
          </h2>

          <p>
            <strong>Every 30-amp RV owner</strong> should carry the Camco PowerGrip 50M/30F dogbone, because many campground pedestals offer 50-amp receptacles and their 30-amp outlets are often the most worn. Add the 15M/30F for driveway use and campsites with only household outlets. That pair, plus a protector, covers almost every hookup you will meet.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>For protection, buy the Progressive Industries EMS-PT30X</strong> if your budget allows. It is the reference portable EMS for 30-amp rigs, it disconnects for the low-voltage, high-voltage and open-neutral problems that cause real damage, and the display tells you what the pedestal is doing. If you prefer to monitor from your phone, the Hughes Power Watchdog in an EMS-equipped version is the alternative. If money is very tight, the Progressive SSP-30XL is a reasonable surge-only start, but plan to upgrade.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>For powering small loads from a generator or pedestal,</strong> the Camco PowerGrip 30M/15F lets you plug a household cord or a 15-amp device into a TT-30 receptacle. It is handy for running a space heater in a workshop from a generator's RV outlet, or a tool at a site with only an RV hookup, but it is limited to 15 amps, so treat it as a small-load adapter.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>For home hookups,</strong> skip clever adapters entirely. A licensed electrician can install a TT-30 or 14-50 receptacle on a dedicated breaker in an afternoon, and it is the only setup we recommend for regular use, especially if you want to run the air conditioner. Do not adapt the RV to a dryer outlet unless an electrician has verified the circuit and the adapter is purpose-built, and never backfeed a house from an RV or generator.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>For 50-amp rigs,</strong> mirror the list: a 50-amp EMS, a 30M/50F dogbone for 30-amp sites and a 15M/50F adapter for driveway charging. If you also want backup power for the RV or the house, start with our <a href="/best-home-generator-for-power-outages/">home generator guide</a> and the <a href="/best-rv-generator/">RV generator picks</a>, and remember the bonding plug for direct generator-to-RV connections. The best RV power adapter setup is the boring one: the right shapes, a working EMS, and no surprises at the pedestal.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            10. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Thinking an adapter adds capacity</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A 15M/30F adapter lets a 30-amp RV plug into a house outlet, but you still only have 15 amps. Running the air conditioner with the microwave or water heater will trip the breaker, or worse, overheat a worn outlet. Manage loads, and have an electrician install a dedicated RV receptacle if you need more.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Adapting an RV to a dryer outlet</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A dryer outlet supplies 240 volts across its hot legs, and older 10-30 outlets lack a separate ground. A wrong adapter can put 240 volts into 120-volt appliances or remove your shock protection. Do not use a dryer outlet unless an electrician has verified the circuit, and prefer a dedicated TT-30 or 14-50.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying a surge-only protector and calling it done</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Surge-only units absorb spikes, but they do not disconnect on low voltage, high voltage or an open neutral, which are the faults that most often damage RV air conditioners and electronics. If your rig has expensive appliances, buy an EMS such as the Progressive EMS-PT30X or an EMS-equipped Power Watchdog.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Blaming the EMS when it rejects a generator</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Many portable generators have a floating neutral, so an EMS reads an open ground and refuses to connect. That is correct behavior. When the generator powers the RV directly, a neutral-ground bonding plug usually solves it; follow the generator maker's guidance, and never use one on a generator feeding a house.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Plugging in with the pedestal breaker on</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Connecting or disconnecting a 30- or 50-amp plug under load creates an arc that pits and burns the blades, and burned blades overheat later. Switch the pedestal breaker off, connect the protector, adapter and cord, then switch it on. Reverse the order when leaving, and replace any discolored adapter.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

