        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            11. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the best RV power adapter?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For most 30-amp RVs, the best RV power adapter setup is the Camco PowerGrip 50M/30F dogbone for 50-amp pedestals and the 15M/30F for household outlets, used behind an EMS protector such as the Progressive Industries EMS-PT30X. Adapters only change plug shapes, so the protector is the part that actually keeps the RV safe. Fifty-amp owners need the 50-amp equivalents.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can I plug my 30 amp RV into a regular outlet?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes, with a 15M/30F 30 amp to 110 adapter, but you are limited to the outlet's 15 amps, or 20 on a 20-amp circuit. That runs lights, the fridge, the water pump and battery charging comfortably. A rooftop air conditioner may run alone on a good circuit but usually not with anything else. Avoid extension cords on the 15-amp side and check the plug for heat.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is a dogbone adapter?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                A dogbone adapter is a short, flexible pigtail with a plug on one end and a receptacle on the other, named for its shape. It converts between RV plug types, such as a 50-amp pedestal to a 30-amp RV cord. Compared with a rigid block adapter, it keeps the weight of the RV cord from levering on the receptacle, which reduces loose connections and heat.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Do I need an RV surge protector?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes. Campground power is often unreliable: overloaded pedestals sag in voltage, miswired outlets show up regularly, and lightning surges happen. An RV surge protector is inexpensive compared with a replacement converter, air conditioner or refrigerator board. We recommend an EMS model that disconnects on low or high voltage and wiring faults, not just a surge-only unit.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Why does my EMS show an open ground on my generator?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Many portable and inverter generators have a floating neutral, meaning neutral and ground are not bonded inside the generator. An EMS expects to see them bonded and reports an open ground. When the generator powers the RV directly, a neutral-ground bonding plug inserted in a spare outlet usually fixes it. Follow your generator's manual, and remove the plug before connecting that generator to a house transfer switch.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can I use a 50 amp RV on a 30 amp outlet?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes, with a 30M/50F dogbone, but both legs of the RV share one 30-amp, 120-volt supply. You are limited to about 3,600 watts in total, which usually means one air conditioner and careful use of other big loads. Your 50-amp EMS still protects the RV, and the RV's energy management panel, if it has one, can help shed loads automatically.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is a TT-30 outlet the same as a dryer outlet?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                No. A TT-30 is a 120-volt, 30-amp RV receptacle with one hot, one neutral and one ground. Dryer outlets, NEMA 10-30 and 14-30, supply 240 volts and may lack a separate ground. They look similar but are not interchangeable, and adapting between them incorrectly can damage the RV or create a shock hazard. Have a licensed electrician install a proper RV receptacle.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
        </div>

